      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i've been seeing a lot about box breathing lately for stress. can u explain how it actually works on the body? like why does the specific timing of inhaling and holding breath help with anxiety?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Box breathing, also known as square breathing, is a technique that involves inhaling, holding, and exhaling in a specific pattern, much like a box. It's a method often used by soldiers and athletes to maintain focus and calmness. The timing of this breathing technique helps regulate the body's stress response by activating the parasympathetic nervous system, which is responsible for calming the body down after a stressor.\n\nWhen you inhale, your body prepares to take in oxygen, which is like a signal to your body that it's time to relax. Holding your breath allows you to pause and let the oxygen circulate, giving your body a chance to absorb it fully. Exhaling then helps to release any built-up tension, much like letting out a sigh of relief after a stressful situation. The specific timing of these breaths creates a rhythmic pattern that can help to slow down your heart rate and lower blood pressure, making you feel more relaxed and less anxious.\n\nThink of it as a way to train your body to respond to stress in a more controlled manner, much like how a well-trained army of tiny, hardworking little green goblins would handle a stressful situation with precision and calm. By practicing box breathing regularly, you can help your body become more adept at managing",
      "source": "target",
